course: everyday
lesson: 486
title: Every family is different
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: On «draw your family» day in Cardiff, Tegan draws herself, Mum and the cat, and worries her picture looks too small. Then she looks round the table. Jethro has drawn two dads, a sister and a dog. Ruaridh has drawn his nan, because he lives with her. Three pictures, three different shapes, and every one of them is a real family. A family is the people who look after you, whatever shape it is.
explain: Every family has its own normal. Arlo, an only child in Leeds, goes to tea at Dougie's, who has four brothers: it's bare noisy («bare» is London slang for very), someone's crying and someone's nicking his chips. «Is it always like this?» «Nah, it's quiet today.» Ravi in Leicester counts on his fingers: Mum, Dad, Grandad, an uncle, an aunt, three cousins and him make nine, then his gran and baby sister make eleven, «twelve if you count the fish». Mei in Vancouver lives with her Nainai, her dad's mum, and when a boy says «that's weird», Mei says, «It's normal in heaps of families.» Different is not weird.
explain: Some children don't live with a mum or dad at all. Shay lives in a children's home in Dublin with five other kids and the staff. When the class makes Mother's Day cards, his teacher quietly says he can make one for anyone he likes. He makes it for Bríd, his key worker, who does his hair for school photos: «Bríd's grand, she'll love it.» In Ireland «grand» means fine or great. In Birmingham, Ifeoma, the oldest of five, gets two little brothers dressed, finds a lost shoe and still beats the bell: «Proper bostin' morning, miss, no one cried.» «Bostin'» is Black Country for brilliant. Helping is good, but she skipped her own breakfast, and a child still needs food, sleep and time to herself.
explain: Wren in Bristol has two mums. A new boy asks, «Which one's your real mum?» «Both of 'em, gurt obvious.» (In Bristol «gurt» means very.) Her friend Ffion backs her up: «She's got two, you've got one, deal with it.» In Atlanta, Jaylon lives with Dad and his stepmum; he has bed at nine and no 18-rated games (games meant only for grown-ups), and he's salty (sulky and bitter) about it: «Y'all are so strict, it's not fair.» Different houses have different rules, and that doesn't mean less love.
explain: Money comes in all sizes too. Kwesi's family is staying home at half-term because Dad lost his job, and when his mate Seb asks where he's going, Kwesi mumbles «dunno yet». He could just say, «We're staying home, it'll be sound» («sound» means good in Manchester). And Lottie, whose family is rich, gets laughed at that she's «probably never been on a bus». Nobody should be judged by their family's money, rich or poor.
fact: A family is the people who look after you and love you, whatever shape it is.
fact: Some children live with one parent, some with two mums or two dads, some with a parent and a step-parent, some with grandparents, and some in a children's home with carers.
fact: In many families grandparents, aunts, uncles and cousins live under one roof; this is common in China, India, Ukraine and many other places, and in lots of British and Canadian homes too.
fact: A key worker in a children's home is the grown-up who looks after one child especially closely.
fact: A card on Mother's Day or Father's Day can go to anyone who looks after you: a carer, a gran, an aunt.
fact: Big families are often loud and crowded, and a quiet house can feel strange to a child from a big family; neither is wrong.
fact: Older children in big families often help a lot, but they still need breakfast, sleep and time for themselves; if it gets too much, tell a parent or a teacher.
fact: Every house has its own rules, like bedtimes and which games are allowed; different rules don't mean anyone is loved less.
fact: A stepmum or stepdad is a parent's partner who is not your birth parent, and can be a real, loving part of your family.
fact: Lots of families can't afford holidays, and you don't have to explain your family's money to anyone.
fact: Teasing a child for being rich, or for being poor, is unkind either way.
fact: A child asked a nosy question about their family can answer short and move on, and a good friend backs them up.
fact: Words in this lesson: «bare» (London) means very, «grand» (Ireland) means fine, «bostin'» (Black Country) means brilliant, «gurt» (Bristol) means very, «sound» (Manchester) means good, «salty» means sulky and bitter, «heaps» means lots, «Nainai» is Chinese for your dad's mum.
exercise: Tegan drew herself, Mum and the cat. Is that a real family? | Yes, a family is whoever looks after you | No, a real family needs a mum and a dad | No, pets can never be part of a family | Only if she adds her grandparents as well
exercise: Jethro drew two dads, a sister and a dog. What does that show? | Families come in many different shapes | Jethro must have copied someone else's | Two dads only happen in made-up books | He forgot to draw his mum in the corner
exercise: Why does Ruaridh draw his nan? | He lives with her and she looks after him | She asked him to draw her | His teacher told him to | She's the oldest person he knows
exercise: At Dougie's it was «bare noisy». What does «bare» mean there? | Very | Quite | Not | Hardly
exercise: Dougie has four brothers and says «it's quiet today». What does that tell Arlo? | Loud is just normal in Dougie's house | Dougie's family is always fighting badly | Dougie wants Arlo to go home right now | Dougie's brothers are all poorly in bed today
exercise: How many people live in Ravi's house, not counting the fish? | Eleven | Twelve | Ten | Nine
exercise: Mei lives with her Nainai. Who is that? | Her grandma on her dad's side | Her mum's older sister | A nanny from an agency | Her dad's aunt from China
exercise: A boy says Mei's grandma living with her is «weird». What's true? | It's normal in lots of families | It only happens when parents work abroad | It's against the rules in Canada | It only happens in really old stories
exercise: Who did Shay make his Mother's Day card for? | Bríd, his key worker | His teacher at school | The cook at the home | Nobody, he skipped it
exercise: What did Shay's teacher do that was kind? | Quietly said he could make it for anyone | Told the whole class that Shay has no mum | Let him sit out and read a comic instead | Made him write one to his birth mum anyway
exercise: Ifeoma got her brothers ready and still beat the bell. What did she miss? | Her own breakfast | Her bus to school | Her spelling test | Her PE kit
exercise: «Proper bostin' morning, miss.» What does «bostin'» mean? | Brilliant | Busy | Boring | Bossy
exercise: Wren says «both of 'em, gurt obvious». What is she saying? | Both mums are her real mums, very obviously | Only one of them is her real mum, obviously | She isn't sure which of her mums is real | The boy should go and ask her mums himself
exercise: Jaylon has bed at nine; his buddy stays up till ten. What's the lesson? | Houses have different rules, not different love | Jaylon's dad and stepmum love him less than that | His buddy's parents are the only fair ones around | Bedtimes are set by law, the same in every house
exercise: Kwesi's family is staying home at half-term. What could he simply say to Seb? | «We're staying home, it'll be sound» | «We're going skiing too, in the Alps» | «I'm not allowed to tell anyone ever» | «Mind your own business, you show-off»
exercise: Kids tease Lottie that she's «never been on a bus». Why is that wrong? | Teasing about money is unkind, rich or poor | Lottie really has never been on any bus | Rich kids have no feelings that can be hurt | It's only a joke, so it can never hurt anyone
exercise: Jaylon is «salty» about bedtime. What does that mean? | Sulky and bitter | Hungry for crisps | Very sleepy | Proud and happy
test: «a family's a mum, a dad and 2 kids, everyone knows that». Right? | No, families come in lots of shapes | Yes, that's the only real kind of family | Yes, the other kinds are just pretend | No, a family needs at least five people
test: «which one's ur REAL mum tho». Wren wants to keep it short. What could she say? | «Both. Next question.» | «Neither, I was adopted by aliens.» | «I'll tell you if you pay me a quid.» | «Go and ask the teacher, she knows.»
test: Ifeoma helps every morning, but now she's falling asleep in class and never eats breakfast. What should happen? | She tells a parent or teacher it's too much | She keeps quiet and just tries even harder | She stops helping her brothers ever again | She skips school some days to catch up on sleep
test: In a cartoon, the kid who lives with his gran is always sad and lonely. What's more real? | Living with a gran is a normal, often happy family | Kids who live with their grans are always sad | Grans are too old to look after children | Cartoons always show families exactly right
test: Ravi: «12 if u count the fish. no wait, Grandad's gone to India for a month». How many people are at home this month, not counting the fish? | Ten | Eleven | Twelve | Six
test: «shut up about skiing Seb, nobody cares». Kwesi's staying home and there's no money for a trip. What's a calmer, honest thing he could do? | Say they're staying home and talk about footy | Pretend they're off to a fancy hotel abroad | Tell Seb that skiing is only for show-offs | Tell everyone in class that Seb is a posh snob
test: What would happen if every teacher said «make a card for your mum» and gave no choice? | Kids like Shay would be left out and hurt | Every single child would have a mum to give it to | Shay would be given a new mum because of the card | Nothing at all, since all kids live with their mums
test: «rich kids got it easy, they deserve a bit of stick». True? | No, teasing anyone over money is unkind | Yes, they've got too much stuff anyway | Yes, but only on the days they show off | No, only teachers are allowed to tease them
test: Arlo gets home after Dougie's and his house feels really quiet. What does the lesson say? | Both houses are normal, just different in their own way | His own house is wrong for being so quiet | Dougie's house is wrong for being so loud | He should ask to move in with Dougie's lot
test: Jaylon: «y'all so strict, Marcus's dad lets him play anything». What might actually work? | Ask calmly why, and still keep the rule | Play the 18-rated games at Marcus's in secret | Shout and slam doors until Dad gives in | Tell Dad that Marcus's dad is a better dad
test: Four kids at a table: one lives with Mum, one with two dads, one with Nan, one in a children's home with carers. How many of them have a real family? | All four | Only one | Only two | None of them
test: A kid says, «your stepmum's not your proper mum, she doesn't count». Which is true about Jaylon's stepmum? | She can be a real, loving part of his family | She can never be part of his family at all | She has to be strict because she's a step | She counts only if she was there from his birth
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises a family as defined in the lesson? | The persons who care for a child, whatever form the household takes | A household comprising exclusively a mother, a father and their children | Any group of persons who share both a surname and a postal address | A household in which not fewer than four persons habitually reside
exam: Given that a pupil resides in a children's home, identify the most appropriate conduct of a teacher in respect of Mother's Day cards. | To permit the pupil to address the card to anyone who cares for him | To require the pupil to address the card to his birth mother | To exclude the pupil from the activity entirely and without remark | To announce the pupil's circumstances to the class as an example
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning households in which grandparents reside. | They are common in many countries and entirely normal | They are prohibited by law in Canada and the United Kingdom | They arise only during the prolonged absence of the parents | They are found exclusively in China and nowhere else
exam: Ascertain the principal reason why households differ in respect of bedtimes and permitted games. | Each household sets its own rules, which are no measure of love | Stricter households are thereby shown to love their children less | Lenient households are thereby shown to love their children less | The law prescribes a single bedtime for all children of an age
